## 6. Functional Requirements

## 6.1 Study Sessions and XP

FR-1 … FR-16 from the initial document are confirmed requirements and are implemented as written, with these concrete implementation specifics:

| ID | Requirement (as built) |
|---|---|
| FR-1 | The application allows logging preset durations 30 minutes, 1 hour, and 2 hours. Presets `(30, 60, 120)` minutes are defined in constants with labels "30 minutes / 1 hour / 2 hours"; buttons log immediately via `log_preset_session(source="preset")`. |
| FR-2 | The application allows logging a custom duration using hours and minutes via a modal dialog. |
| FR-3 | Custom duration entry provides separate fields for hours and minutes. |
| FR-4 | Custom duration is valid only if total duration is at least 5 minutes and no more than 12 hours; invalid values are rejected with a friendly message. |
| FR-5 | Minutes input accepts 0–59; hours input accepts 0–12. Validation runs through `Session.validate_custom_inputs(hours, minutes)` returning `(total_minutes, friendly_error_or_None)`. |
| FR-6 | When a session is logged, it is timestamped with a start datetime and an end datetime: `Session.create()` stamps end = current local time (or injected `now` for tests), start = end − duration, plus `created_at_local` and `source` ("preset"/"custom"). Naive local datetimes throughout. |
| FR-7 | Each session is attributed to the calendar day on which its start datetime falls (`Session.day = start_local.date()`). |
| FR-8 | Sessions that cross midnight are not split across calendar days; no splitting exists anywhere in the codebase. |
| FR-9 | XP is awarded at the configured rate of **10 XP per completed 10 minutes**; the rate is a persisted application setting without a v1 UI editor. |
| FR-10 | XP is calculated only from completed 10-minute blocks: `(duration // 10) * settings.xp_per_10_minutes`; partial blocks round down. |
| FR-11 | XP values are whole numbers; no fractional XP is displayed or stored. |
| FR-12 | Total session duration counts toward daily medal thresholds even if the session is too short to earn XP; medal/time engines use raw `duration_minutes`, independent of XP rounding. |
| FR-13 | The user can delete an existing session (editing remains out of scope). Delete flows through `AppState.delete_session` → index update → autosave → listener broadcast refreshing every built screen (Home, Calendar, Stats, Notes, open Day Details). |
| FR-14 | Deleting a session requires confirmation. |
| FR-15 | Deleting a session immediately updates daily totals, medals, calendar display, statistics, and streaks (derived values must not remain stale). |
| FR-16 | The application does not prevent overlapping sessions; no overlap detection exists. |
| **[UPDATED]** | Manual-load validation additionally rejects nonsensical persisted durations (≤0 or >24 h) by skipping those records rather than crashing (see ERR-10/ERR-21). |

### XP Calculation Examples

These examples assume the confirmed default rate of 10 XP per completed 10 minutes.

| Session Duration | Completed 10-Minute Blocks | XP Awarded | Notes |
|---:|---:|---:|---|
| 5 minutes | 0 | 0 | Still counts toward medal time. |
| 10 minutes | 1 | 10 | Minimum XP-earning duration. |
| 25 minutes | 2 | 20 | Remaining 5 minutes are ignored for XP. |
| 30 minutes | 3 | 30 | Preset duration. |
| 1 hour | 6 | 60 | Preset duration. |
| 2 hours | 12 | 120 | Preset duration. |
| 2 hours 9 minutes | 12 | 120 | Extra 9 minutes do not create another block. |

### Midnight-Crossing Examples

| Scenario | Attribution Rule |
|---|---|
| User logs a 30-minute session at 00:10. The calculated start time is 23:40 on the previous day. | The full session is attributed to the previous day. |
| User logs a 2-hour session from 23:30 to 01:30. | The full session is attributed to the day on which it started. |
| User logs a 1-hour session at 00:20. The calculated start time is 23:20 on the previous day. | The full session is attributed to the previous day. |

## 6.2 Daily Medals

FR-17 … FR-26 are confirmed requirements, implemented as written: inclusive thresholds 120/180/300 minutes, highest-medal-only display and counting, derived-not-stored state, and automatic recalculation/downgrade on add and delete (the daily index recomputes `medal_for_minutes(total)` on every mutation). Medal keys/labels/asset names are enumerated centrally (`bronze/silver/gold` → `*_medal` assets).

| ID | Requirement | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| FR-17 | The application awards daily medals based on total study time attributed to a calendar day. | Total time is the sum of all sessions attributed to that day. |
| FR-18 | Bronze medal requires at least 2 hours of study in one day. | 120 minutes (`BRONZE_MIN_MINUTES`). |
| FR-19 | Silver medal requires at least 3 hours of study in one day. | 180 minutes (`SILVER_MIN_MINUTES`). |
| FR-20 | Gold medal requires at least 5 hours of study in one day. | 300 minutes (`GOLD_MIN_MINUTES`). |
| FR-21 | Medal thresholds are inclusive. | Exactly 2 hours earns Bronze; exactly 3 hours earns Silver; exactly 5 hours earns Gold. |
| FR-22 | If a user qualifies for multiple medals on the same day, only the highest earned medal is displayed and counted. | Example: 5 hours earns Gold only. |
| FR-23 | Days with less than 2 hours of study display no medal. | No participation medal in v1. |
| FR-24 | Medal state is derived from current session data. | Medals are not independent permanent records. |
| FR-25 | Medals are recalculated automatically whenever sessions are added or deleted. | |
| FR-26 | If a deleted session causes a day to fall below a medal threshold, the medal is removed or downgraded automatically. | Calendar and statistics must update. |

### Medal Threshold Table

| Medal | Minimum Study Time | Display Priority |
|---|---:|---:|
| Bronze | 2 hours | Lower than Silver/Gold |
| Silver | 3 hours | Lower than Gold |
| Gold | 5 hours | Highest |

